  "warnings": [
    "lineage agent notes: - Rows: the output has one row per (accident_year, lob_code) group that has at least one qualifying payment. The INNER JOIN to stg.claim_payments and the payment filters on lines 92-94 decide which groups appear, so claims with no qualifying payments drop out. The PIVOT groups only by accident_year and lob_code, because the source subquery selects only those two columns, dev_year and amount (claim_id and payment_id are left out).\n- dev_n / cumulative_n: payment_date and loss_date are listed as derives edges because they pick the pivot bucket a payment lands in: dev_year = DATEDIFF(YEAR, loss_date, payment_date), which counts calendar-year boundaries, not full years. You could argue they are filters instead; their confidence is lower for that reason. A payment made later in the same calendar year as the loss is in dev_0. dev_year can't fall outside 0..6 given the filters.\n- dev_1..dev_6 are NULL when accident_year + n > YEAR(@AsOfDate). The cumulative columns are filled by a later UPDATE (lines 144-151) that adds up the stored dev_n values. With ordinary + arithmetic, a NULL dev cell makes every later cumulative NULL too, which is the intended behavior.\n- The cumulative columns are left out of the INSERT column list, so they are NULL until the UPDATE runs. A migration must match the final state after the UPDATE.\n- lob_code is trimmed and upper-cased before grouping (UPPER(RTRIM)), so lob_code values that differ only in case or trailing spaces end up in the same group.\n- Both stg tables are read WITH (NOLOCK). The legacy output can therefore be non-deterministic if the inputs change during the run."
  ]
